  "articles": [
    {
      "title": "About the Hebrew alphabet",
      "body": "<p>The Hebrew letters in use today, called the square script, developed from the Aramaic alphabet over 2,000 years ago. They are used for Hebrew and also for Yiddish and Ladino, which give some letters different values.</p>\n<p>Hebrew is written right to left, with no capital letters. Like Arabic, it is an abjad: the letters write consonants, and readers supply most of the vowels from their knowledge of the language. The letters <span lang=\"he\">א, ה, ו, י</span> can also stand for vowels.</p>\n<p>Each letter has a number value (<span lang=\"he\">א</span> = 1, <span lang=\"he\">ב</span> = 2, <span lang=\"he\">י</span> = 10 …), still used for dates in the Hebrew calendar and for numbering lists.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Reading without vowels",
      "body": "<p>Everyday Hebrew is written without vowel points. The word <span lang=\"he\">ספר</span> could be <span lang=\"he\">סֵפֶר</span> (sefer, book), <span lang=\"he\">סַפָּר</span> (sapar, barber) or <span lang=\"he\">סִפֵּר</span> (siper, he told). Readers know which from the context.</p>\n<p>Two habits make unpointed text easier. <span lang=\"he\">ו</span> often shows an o or u, and <span lang=\"he\">י</span> an i: <span lang=\"he\">שיר</span> is shir, <span lang=\"he\">טוב</span> is tov. Learn new words with their points, then practise reading them without.</p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Letters that look alike",
      "body": "<p>Several pairs differ by a small detail:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td lang=\"he\">ב כ</td><td>bet has a little foot at the bottom right; kaf is rounded</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"he\">ד ר</td><td>dalet has a sharp corner; resh is rounded</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"he\">ה ח ת</td><td>he has a gap at the top left; het is closed; tav has a foot at the bottom left</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"he\">ו ז ן</td><td>vav is a plain stroke; zayin has a head; final nun goes below the line</td></tr>\n<tr><td lang=\"he\">ס ם</td><td>samekh is round; final mem is square</td></tr>\n</table>"
    }
  ]
